# Permissions & Roles

If the application has authentication and roles, centralize permission definitions.

Example:

```javascript
export const USER_ROLES = {
  ADMIN: "admin",
  MANAGER: "manager",
  INVENTORY_STAFF: "inventory_staff",
  VIEWER: "viewer",
};
```

Example permissions:

```text
inventory.view
inventory.create
inventory.update
inventory.delete
inventory.adjust
inventory.transfer
inventory.export
products.manage
suppliers.manage
reports.view
```

### Do

* Check permissions before displaying protected actions.
* Protect routes where required.
* Reuse permission helpers.
* Keep authorization enforced by the backend.

### Don't

* Don't rely only on hiding buttons for security.
* Don't duplicate role strings throughout the application.
* Don't implement authorization only on the frontend.

---

# Loading States

Every async operation must have appropriate feedback.

Examples:

```text
Table Skeleton
Page Skeleton
Button Spinner
Dialog Loading State
Form Submit Loading
Bulk Action Loading
```

Example:

```jsx
<Button disabled={loading}>
  {loading ? "Saving..." : "Save Product"}
</Button>
```

Don't allow users to accidentally submit the same operation multiple times.

---

# Error States

Use a consistent error experience.

Examples:

```text
ErrorState
Inline Form Error
Toast Notification
Retry Button
```

For page-level failures:

```jsx
<ErrorState
  title="Unable to load inventory"
  message={error}
  onRetry={fetchInventory}
/>
```

Don't expose unnecessary technical details to users.

---

# Empty States

Empty states should explain what happened and, where useful, provide an action.

Examples:

```text
No products found.
No inventory available.
No suppliers added yet.
No stock movements found.
```

Example:

```jsx
<EmptyState
  title="No products found"
  description="Add your first product to start managing inventory."
  action={<Button>Add Product</Button>}
/>
```

